title: Streamed tool_use in Anthropic::Stream
updated: 2026-08-11T22:52:01Z
---

Anthropic::Stream currently does NOT honour streaming tool calls. finish_reason=tool_calls maps stop_reason to tool_use, but no tool_use block is emitted in the stream — so an Anthropic client doing real work (Claude Code, Cursor, anything that invokes tools) sees:

1. message_start + content_block_start (type=text)
2. deltas with text
3. content_block_stop
4. message_delta with stop_reason=tool_use
5. message_stop

No tool_use content block. stop_reason tool_use with empty content array. Clients cannot recover from this — they have to assume the model hung.

The OpenAI stream-side is fine: tool_calls.id, tool_calls.function.name, tool_calls.function.arguments all arrive in pieces across deltas and the Anthropic mapping just has to emit them as the right Anthropic events at the right moments.

Fix has three pieces:
- Track a streaming tool_use block index (one Anthropic event stream can carry multiple tool_use blocks in parallel — common with agents that fan out a parallel search).
- On the first delta that mentions a new tool_call id, emit content_block_start(type=tool_use, id=...toolu_xxx, name=...), opening a new content block.
- On each subsequent delta with arguments for that id, emit content_block_delta(type=input_json_delta, partial_json=...).
- On the final chunk, close the block with content_block_stop.

Same fix is what Langertha already models for non-streaming responses (the Anthropic tool_use block has type, id, name, input); this is just expressing it through SSE events instead of a single JSON message.

Precondition for karr #22 option B (t/34-agent-flow-tools.t). Land it first or in the same commit; agent-flow text-only (#22 option A) does NOT need it and can proceed regardless.

Done-when:
- Anthropic::Stream can carry a tool_call split across multiple deltas
- the resulting Anthropic event sequence starts with content_block_start(type=tool_use) for every tool and ends each with content_block_stop
- message_delta.usage.output_tokens covers the tool_use text plus any prose
- the protocol worker's report on karr #8 covers this (their report will mention the gap; close it here)
- prove -lr t/ green

Why this is a separate ticket, not folded into #8: #8 is closing a stale-POD gap, this is a feature addition. Mixing them would conflate cleanup with new behaviour.
